WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.430
Early morning.

2
00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:07.050
Many Sikhs are already reciting a long
poem they know by heart: the Japji.

3
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:11.070
It opens the Sikh scripture,
on the very first page.

4
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.040
And that page begins with the numeral 1.

5
00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:19.380
Yet in many translations, the poem soon
says God cannot be reached by thinking,

6
00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:22.190
not even a hundred thousand times.

7
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:26.910
So why would Sikhs begin each day with
a poem that says thinking cannot reach

8
00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.020
God?

9
00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.900
In this mission, you'll
decode the Mool Mantar,

10
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:34.170
the poem's opening lines, word by word.

11
00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:39.080
Then you'll walk through its 38 stanzas,
and see where Sikhs recite them.

12
00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:44.370
Your mission: find out why Sikhs start the
day with a poem that says God is beyond

13
00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:45.170
thinking.
